tel 1.0

base prophesy
import soundness

info   the vocabulary of code completion: candidates, keyword contexts and elaborated call sites
detail
    _Prophesy_ holds the types a code-completion engine and the libraries it serves share,
    without either depending on the compiler. A completion engine such as Harlequin's
    `Scala.highlight(…).completions` produces `Completions`; a `Dynamic` type offers the
    members its `selectDynamic` would accept by giving its companion object the `Completable`
    interface; and keyword completion is answered by `ScalaKeywords.pattern`, a curated trie
    over the tokens leading back from the caret, abstracted as `Lexeme`s.

    A `Dynamic` type whose valid member names are not symbols can still complete honestly:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import prophesy.*

    trait Creature extends Dynamic:
      def selectDynamic(name: String): Text = t""

    object Creature extends Completable:
      def completions(using quotes: scala.quoted.Quotes)
        ( receiver: quotes.reflect.TypeRepr, prefix: Text )
      :   List[Completion] =

        import quotes.reflect.*

        List(t"habitat", t"diet").map: name =>
          Completion(name, Completion.Kind.Term, Syntax(TypeRepr.of[Text]))
    ```

    The pieces fit as follows:

    - `Completion` and `Completions` are one candidate and one answer to a completion request.
    - `Completable` is the interface a `Dynamic` type's companion implements to offer members.
    - `Lexeme`, `KeywordPattern`, `Keywords` and `ScalaKeywords` answer which keywords may come
      next at a caret, and what non-keyword content the grammar expects there.
    - `Elaboration` records what the typer added to a call beyond its source text — inferred
      type arguments and synthesized `using` arguments — for hover and inlay hints.

    The library has one source set, `prophesy.core`. Only `Completable` is exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella: `Completion` and `Completions` would clash there with
    exoskeleton's command-line completions of the same names, so they are reached as
    `prophesy.Completion` and `prophesy.Completions` (and Harlequin re-exports them in its own
    package). The keyword and elaboration types are reached through `import prophesy.*`.

refer prophesy.Completable
refer prophesy.Completion
refer prophesy.ScalaKeywords

entry .Completable   the interface through which a `Dynamic` type's companion offers tab completions
  detail
      The compiler's interactive completion engine cannot enumerate the members a
      `selectDynamic` or `applyDynamic` implementation would accept, but a refined receiver
      type often determines them fully. A `Dynamic` type offers them by having its companion
      object extend `Completable`. A completion engine (such as Harlequin's) finds the
      companion from the receiver's type at the caret, loads it reflectively in its own JVM
      and calls `#completions` with a live `Quotes` and the receiver's full, refined
      `TypeRepr`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import prophesy.*

      trait Creature extends Dynamic:
        def selectDynamic(name: String): Text = t""

      object Creature extends Completable:
        def completions(using quotes: scala.quoted.Quotes)
          ( receiver: quotes.reflect.TypeRepr, prefix: Text )
        :   List[Completion] =

          import quotes.reflect.*

          List(t"habitat", t"diet").map: name =>
            Completion(name, Completion.Kind.Term, Syntax(TypeRepr.of[Text]))
      ```

      Because the companion is loaded reflectively from the classes on the engine's
      classpath, it must be compiled and on that classpath, not merely in the source being
      edited. This is how a xenophile `Foreign` value completes with the members its foreign
      declarations name. `Completable` is exported to the `soundness` umbrella.
  refer prophesy.Completion
  entry #completions   the completions for a receiver type and the partial member name typed so far
      Takes the receiver's `TypeRepr` and the `prefix` already typed at the caret (possibly
      empty), and returns a `List[Completion]`. An implementation may filter by the prefix
      but need not, since the engine filters by it regardless. It must be pure and total: any
      exception it throws is caught by the engine and treated as no completions.

entry .Completion   one completion candidate: the text to insert, its kind, its signature and documentation
  detail
      A case class of `#name`, `#kind`, `#signature` and an optional `#documentation`. A
      completion engine returns a list of them inside `Completions`, and a `Completable`
      companion builds them for the members of a `Dynamic` type.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import prophesy.*

      def candidate(using quotes: scala.quoted.Quotes): Completion =
        import quotes.reflect.*
        Completion(t"habitat", Completion.Kind.Term, Syntax(TypeRepr.of[Text]), t"where it lives")
      ```

      `Completion` is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella, where exoskeleton's
      command-line completions already use the name; write `prophesy.Completion`, or import
      it from `prophesy`. Harlequin re-exports it as `harlequin.Completion`.
  refer prophesy.Completions
  refer prophesy.Completable
  entry .Kind   what sort of member a completion candidate is
      An enum with the cases `Term`, `Method`, `Given`, `Extension`, `Type`, `Module`,
      `Package` and `Keyword`, which an editor uses to choose an icon and to decide, for
      instance, whether to insert parentheses after a `Method`.
  entry #name   the text to insert
  entry #kind   the candidate's `Completion.Kind`: term, method, given, extension, type, module, package or keyword
  entry #signature   the member's type as a stenography `Syntax`: a method's full signature, or a value's type
  entry #documentation   the member's documentation, or `Unset` if it has none

entry .Completions   the answer to a completion request: the region to replace and the candidates
  detail
      `#replace` is the source region the chosen completion replaces, as an `Offset`-mode
      `Span` — the partial identifier at the caret — and `#items` the candidates.
      Harlequin's `Scala.highlight(source, caret = …).completions` returns one, or `Unset`
      when no caret was given.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      given LocalClasspath = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])
      import highlighting.typecheckedScala

      val source = t"val xs = List(1, 2, 3)\nval y = xs.m"
      Scala.highlight(source, caret = source.length.z).completions.lay(Nil)(_.items.map(_.name))
      ```

      Like `Completion`, it is reached as `prophesy.Completions` (or `harlequin.Completions`),
      not through the `soundness` umbrella.
  refer prophesy.Completion
  entry #replace   the `Offset`-mode `Span` of source which a chosen completion replaces
  entry #items   the completion candidates, as `Completion`s

entry .Elaboration   one call site the typer elaborated beyond its source text, for hovers and inlay hints
  detail
      Records, for one call, what the compiler added that the programmer did not write: the
      callee's simple name as `#method`, its token's source offsets as `#start` and `#end`,
      the *inferred* type arguments as `#typeArguments` and the *synthesized* `using`
      arguments as `#givenArguments`. Anything written in the source is never recorded, so an
      editor can show exactly the elaboration that is otherwise invisible.

      `.scan` finds every such call in a typed tree. Harlequin uses it to attach elaborations
      to the tokens of highlighted source, keyed by their offsets.
  refer prophesy.Elaboration.Instance
  entry .Instance   the given the compiler supplied for one synthesized `using` argument
    detail
        `#name` is the qualified name of the given's head symbol — for a derived instance,
        the derivation method's — and `#tpe` the argument's widened type, as a stenography
        `Syntax`.
    entry #name   the qualified name of the supplied given, or of the method which derived it
    entry #tpe   the synthesized argument's widened type, as a `Syntax`
  entry .scan   every elaborated call site in a typed tree, ordered by position
      Takes a `Quotes` and a typed `Tree`, and returns a `List[Elaboration]` sorted by
      `#start`, merging the type and given arguments of the several clauses of one call. The
      scan works equally on a live typer run and on trees unpickled from TASTy: an inferred
      type argument is recognised by spanning the whole function tree it was wrapped around,
      and a synthesized `using` argument by being zero-extent at the end of its clause.

      An `Inlined` node contributes its original call and is not descended into, since the
      expansion's trees carry the inline definition's own positions. The scan never fails: a
      node which cannot be read is skipped. A selection's token is approximated as its
      trailing `name.length` characters, which misplaces backticked names; a misplaced key
      attaches to no token, so the cost is only a missing hint.
  entry #method   the callee's simple name
  entry #start   the source offset at which the callee's token begins
  entry #end   the source offset at which the callee's token ends
  entry #typeArguments   the type arguments the typer inferred, as `Syntax`, omitting any written explicitly
  entry #givenArguments   the `using` arguments the typer synthesized, as `Elaboration.Instance`s

entry .Lexeme   a token abstracted for keyword completion: keywords and symbols kept, identifiers erased
  detail
      The alphabet a `KeywordPattern` matches against: an abstraction of a token stream in
      which identifier text is irrelevant (any term identifier is just `Term`, any type
      identifier `Typal`) but every keyword and every symbolic token is individually
      significant. A tokenizer such as Harlequin's maps its tokens onto lexemes, and the
      pattern is consulted with the sequence *reversed*, reading outwards from the caret.

      The cases are `Keyword(word)`, covering hard keywords and soft modifiers alike,
      identified by their exact text — near a caret a soft modifier is lexically
      indistinguishable from an identifier, so the mapping is by text; `Symbol(glyph)` for a
      symbolic token such as `=>` or `:`; `Open(bracket)` and `Close(bracket)`, with a
      `Lexeme.Bracket`; `Term`, `Typal` and `Literal`; `Break`, a statement boundary (a line
      break whose continuation is not more deeply indented, where a line break which merely
      continues an expression contributes nothing); `Start`, the beginning of the input; and
      `Error`, a token the tokenizer could not classify.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import prophesy.*

      // the caret in `transparent inline |`, read outwards
      val context: List[Lexeme] =
        List(Lexeme.Keyword(t"inline"), Lexeme.Keyword(t"transparent"), Lexeme.Break)
      ```
  refer prophesy.KeywordPattern
  entry .Bracket   the shape of a bracket lexeme: `Round`, `Square` or `Brace`

entry .KeywordPattern   a trie over reversed lexeme contexts, answering which keywords may come next
  detail
      A node of the pattern tree. `#result`, when present, is the `Keywords` determined by the
      context consumed so far; `#branches` refine it by looking one `Lexeme` further back from
      the caret. Lookup, by `#apply`, descends the first branch whose element matches the next
      lexeme and returns the deepest result encountered, so an interior result is the default
      when no deeper pattern matches, and the tree looks back exactly as far as remains
      relevant. Branch order is significance order: `Exact` branches should precede the
      wildcard class branches.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import prophesy.*
      import KeywordPattern.{Element, Expectation}

      val definition = Keywords(Set(t"def", t"given"))

      val tree = KeywordPattern
        ( Unset,
          List
            ( Element.Exact(Lexeme.Keyword(t"inline")) -> KeywordPattern
                ( Keywords(definition.keywords + t"if"),
                  List(Element.Exact(Lexeme.Keyword(t"transparent")) -> KeywordPattern(definition)) ),
              Element.Exact(Lexeme.Symbol(t".")) -> KeywordPattern(Keywords(Set(), Expectation.Nothing)) ) )

      tree(List(Lexeme.Keyword(t"inline"), Lexeme.Keyword(t"transparent")))   // definition
      tree(List(Lexeme.Keyword(t"inline"), Lexeme.Literal))   // the `inline` default, with `if`
      tree(Nil)   // Keywords.empty
      ```

      `ScalaKeywords.pattern` is the curated tree for Scala itself.
  refer prophesy.ScalaKeywords
  refer prophesy.Lexeme
  refer prophesy.Keywords
  entry .Element   one step of a keyword pattern: an exact lexeme, or a class of lexemes
    detail
        The element a branch of a `KeywordPattern` matches against a single lexeme of the
        reversed context. `Exact(lexeme)` matches one lexeme; the class elements are the
        wildcard mechanism, letting a curated tree generalise without enumerating every
        literal context: `AnyKeyword` matches any `Lexeme.Keyword`; `AnySymbol` any
        `Lexeme.Symbol`, the open class of operators such as `+`, `::` and `++`; `ValueEnd`
        any token which ends an expression (a closing bracket, a term identifier or a
        literal); `TypeEnd` any token which ends a type (a type identifier or a closing `]`);
        `AnyOf(lexemes)` any of a set; and `Any` every lexeme.
    entry #matches   whether this element accepts a lexeme
  entry .Expectation   what non-keyword content the grammar expects at the caret
      An enum: `TermBinding`, where a fresh term name is being written (after `val`, `def`,
      or at the head of a parameter), so member completions should be suppressed;
      `TypeBinding`, where a fresh type name is (after `class`, `trait`, `type`);
      `TypeIdentifier`, where only a type may follow (after `new`, `extends`, an ascription's
      `:`), so offers are narrowed to types; and `Nothing`, where no keyword is possible but
      member completions remain valid, as after `.`.
  entry #result   the keywords determined by the context matched so far, or `Unset` to inherit the enclosing node's
  entry #branches   the refinements of this node, each an `Element` and the subtree to descend when it matches
  entry #apply   looks up the keywords for a reversed lexeme context, read outwards from the caret
      Returns the deepest `Keywords` found along the first matching path, or `Keywords.empty`
      if no node on it has a result. The context is consumed only as deep as the tree goes,
      so passing the whole of a long line costs nothing more than passing its last tokens.

entry .Keywords   the keywords plausible at a caret, and what other content the grammar expects there
  detail
      The result of consulting a `KeywordPattern`: the set of `#keywords` which may come next,
      and optionally an `#expectation`, a `KeywordPattern.Expectation` saying whether a fresh
      name, a type, or nothing but members is expected. A multi-word keyword such as
      `case class` appears as a single element of the set.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import prophesy.*

      val found: Keywords = ScalaKeywords.pattern(List(Lexeme.Keyword(t"def")))
      found.expectation == KeywordPattern.Expectation.TermBinding
      ```
  entry .empty   no keywords and no expectation: the result of a lookup which matched nothing
  entry #keywords   the keywords which may come next, as text; a multi-word keyword is one element
  entry #expectation   what non-keyword content is expected, or `Unset` if the pattern does not say

entry .ScalaKeywords   Scala 3's hard and soft keywords, and the curated keyword-completion tree for Scala
  detail
      `.hard` and `.soft` are Scala 3's alphanumeric keywords, and `.all` their union, the
      one copy shared by Harlequin's highlighting and any host which must reject a keyword
      as a fresh name. `.pattern` is the `KeywordPattern` for keyword completion in Scala
      source.

      The pattern is hand-written, derived from and checked against a corpus analysis of
      about 445,000 keyword occurrences in real Scala, which reports the keywords observed
      after every reversed caret context and how far back the context must be read before
      it settles. Soft-modifier noise is pruned from the corpus's sets — `open` after `def` is
      a method name, not a keyword — and grammar the corpus under-attests is added back, such
      as `class` and `object` after `case`. `implicit` and Scala-2-style `with` after `:` are
      never offered. Where one lexeme context covers two grammatical positions, such as an
      indented template body after `:` and a same-line ascription, the union is offered,
      since a keyword offered where it is not strictly valid is harmless but a missing one is
      a failure.

      Here is how the offer narrows as more of the context before the caret is read:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label After `inline`, a definition or an inline conditional
        code
            import soundness.*
            import prophesy.*

            ScalaKeywords.pattern(List(Lexeme.Keyword(t"inline"))).keywords
            // def, given, val, if, match, infix
      frame
        label After `transparent inline`, only a definition
        code
            import soundness.*
            import prophesy.*

            ScalaKeywords.pattern(List(Lexeme.Keyword(t"inline"), Lexeme.Keyword(t"transparent"))).keywords
            // def, given
      frame
        label After `def`, a fresh name and no keywords
        code
            import soundness.*
            import prophesy.*

            ScalaKeywords.pattern(List(Lexeme.Keyword(t"def")))
            // Keywords(Set(), TermBinding)
      ##
      ```
  refer prophesy.KeywordPattern
  refer prophesy.Lexeme
  entry .hard   Scala 3's alphanumeric hard keywords, reserved everywhere and so never identifiers
      From `abstract` to `yield`, including `implicit` and `macro`, as `Set[Text]`.
  entry .soft   Scala 3's alphanumeric soft keywords, which are keywords only in particular positions
      `as`, `derives`, `end`, `erased`, `extension`, `infix`, `inline`, `opaque`, `open`,
      `throws`, `tracked`, `transparent` and `using`: identifiers the parser reads as
      keywords where its lookahead says so, and which a lexer without that lookahead tags as
      plain identifiers. A host deciding whether a word is a name needs them alongside
      `.hard`.
  entry .all   every Scala 3 keyword, hard and soft, as one set
  entry .pattern   the curated `KeywordPattern` for Scala keyword completion
      Consulted with the reversed `Lexeme`s before the caret. Statement boundaries offer
      everything which can begin a statement; a member selection offers only postfix `match`,
      singleton `.type` and import `.given`, with `Expectation.Nothing`; a definition head
      expects a fresh binding and offers no keywords; and each modifier offers what may
      follow it.
